BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre": "https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/ojiichans-dragon-toronto-book-launch"}
X-SOURCE-ID:jccc
SUMMARY:Ojiichan's Dragon Toronto Book Launch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261122T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261122T150000
UID:845c5ccb88c54e0ca90da90cde688791@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Join us at the Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre (JCCC) on Sun
 day\, November 22nd\, 2026 from 1:00pm - 3:00pm ET as we celebrate Jeff Ch
 iba Stearns' newest graphic novel\, Ojiichan's Dragon! Jeff Chiba Stearns 
 is a fourth-generation Japanese Canadian illustrator and author who will s
 hare behind-the-scenes insights on the creative process and making of Ojii
 chan's Dragon. He is an Emmy nominated\, multi-award-winning filmmaker\, i
 llustrator and author. His visual storytelling focuses on themes of histor
 y\, cultural heritage\, and family across animation\, documentary\, and bo
 oks. Ojiichan's Dragon is Jeff's 3rd graphic novel. We will then be joined
  by Erica H. Isomura\, illustrator of the non-fiction children's book Asah
 i: Japanese Canadian Baseball Heroes\, and Dr. Lara Okihiro\, co-author of
  children's novel\, Obaasan's Boots\, for a Japanese Canadian panel discus
 sion and Q&A session. Erica H. Isomura is an artist\, writer\, and communi
 ty educator. Their expansive creative practice combines writing\, bookmaki
 ng\, paper art\, eco-art\, drawing\, printmaking\, collage and mixed-media
  with critical diasporic and land-based storytelling. Erica is a "self-tau
 ght" artist who credits her mom\, aunties\, and 婆婆 as her first teache
 rs in contemporary craft and art. Dr. Lara Okihiro is a yonsei author\, re
 searcher\, and educator with a BA\, MA (Goldsmiths\, UK)\, and PhD (Toront
 o) in literature. Her relationship with her grandparents\, along with livi
 ng abroad\, encouraged her to study the Japanese Canadian internment. Her 
 acclaimed children’s novel\, Obaasan’s Boots\, based on her family’s
  incarceration and co-written with her cousin\, was shortlisted for the BC
  and Yukon Book Prize and Red Cedar Book Award. She’s currently completi
 ng a children’s novel about a mixed-race character in a magical world of
  ancestors and spirits that incorporates her grandfather’s haiku poetry.
  Books will be available for purchase\, and Jeff\, Erica\, and Lara can si
 gn copies of their works after the panel discussion.\n\nCategories: Commun
 ity Event\, Heritage
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/ojiichans-dragon-toronto-book-launch
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
