X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Quilting Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T200000
UID:6a3e8cc6414ef43d00981be7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The focus of this program is to make quilts for donation\, usi
 ng fabrics that have been donated to the group for this purpose.\nParticip
 ants meet weekly to share ideas and inspiration while working on quilts. T
 here are no sewing machines at the library so any sewing is done by hand. 
 Most participants also work on quilts using their own sewing machine at ho
 me. Quilts are completed both as individual projects and as group collabor
 ations.\nNew members are welcome\, and will have access to the instruction
 s\, fabrics\, needle and thread required to contribute on-site to the curr
 ent group project.\nRegarding dates: The quilters will not meet when schoo
 l is out. E.g. March break and during the summer (July and August) and dur
 ing December break.
LOCATION:Jones
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3e8cc6414ef43d00981be7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6adbc6957b0eb473bbdb27b4"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:A Short History of Library Architecture in Toronto and Beyond
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T200000
UID:6adbc6957b0eb473bbdb27b4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Hume\, the architecture critic and urban issues co
 lumnist of the Toronto Star\, presents a short history of library architec
 ture. He will be commenting on the structures and social relevance of monu
 mental libraries from Paris\, Boston\, New York\, with a focus on Toronto 
 Public Libraries from around the city.\nHume is the recipient of 2009 The 
 National Newspaper Award for journalism in architecture and urban affairs.
  His illustrious career at the Toronto Star earned him a President’s Awa
 rd for Architectural Journalism from the Royal Architectural Institute of 
 Canada. Hume's book\, William James’ Toronto Views\, won a Toronto Herit
 age Award in 2000 and in 2004 he received a Landscape Ontario award.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6adbc6957b0eb473bbdb27b4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
